; A tree recovered from a syntax error is not a placeholder — it evaluates.
; `parse-unclosed-form` proves the recovered form is structurally complete
; (its required key is satisfied); this case pushes the same recovered node
; all the way through the evaluator and the envelope encoder, so a host
; cannot honour recovery structurally while quietly refusing to compute on
; the result.
;
; Named into the `expr-trig-*` family deliberately: the value is the whole
; point, and TS-parity's standing reason for skipping that family — "binds
; via `(values …)`, which needs the Expr evaluator" — is exactly why it
; cannot run this one either. Its half of the recovery contract is pinned
; by `parse-unclosed-form` and `parse-recovery-continues`, which it does
; run.
;
; `sin 1.0` is the same input as `expr-trig-sin` form 0, so the expected
; value is that case's, unchanged: recovery must not perturb it.
(sin 1.0
